## DC 5 — Controls Implemented (CC1 – CC9)

Shared governance and review cadence — applies to CC1 – CC9 unless stated otherwise. All policies referenced below are owned by the ISMS Governance Council, reviewed at least annually, and approved by the CEO. Control operation is monitored continuously in Vanta, with evidence collected per control. Identified gaps and findings are tracked to closure as Linear tickets.

### CC1 — Control Environment

- Information Security Policy, Acceptable Use Policy, and Code of Conduct are acknowledged by all employees and contractors at onboarding.
- The ISMS Governance Council is chaired by the CTO (also the Information Security Management Leader and DPO) and includes the COO, business-unit delegates, and engineering leadership.
- Background checks are performed at hire where legally permitted.

### CC2 — Communication and Information

- Internal: information security policies, incident-response procedures, and the change-management process are published on the company intranet.
- External: customer-facing commitments are published in the Terms of Service, SLA, and DPA. A security reporting channel (`security@normain.com`) is published on the Trust Center.

### CC3 — Risk Assessment

- Annual enterprise risk assessment performed by the ISMS Governance Council. Risks are recorded in the risk register with owners, treatment plans, and review dates.
- Fraud risk and changes to the operating environment are considered within the same annual cycle.

### CC4 — Monitoring Activities

- Continuous control monitoring via Vanta covers all CC1 – CC9 controls.
- Independent penetration test conducted at least annually (latest report: 2026 by Cacilian, received 2026-05-18; prior round: May 2025).
- Backup restore tested at least annually (most recent exercise: 2025-07-22 disaster-recovery tabletop simulating production-database deletion, including full restore from Azure-managed automated backup).

### CC5 — Control Activities

- Control selection and design is documented and owned by the ISMS Governance Council. Technical controls are implemented in code, infrastructure-as-code, and SaaS configuration.

### CC6 — Logical and Physical Access

- Access is provisioned via a documented onboarding workflow with role-based permissions. Joiner / mover / leaver events trigger access changes within 1 business day.
- User access is reviewed at least annually; vendor access reviews are performed quarterly (latest cycles: 2025-06-19, 2025-10-12, 2026-04-29).
- End-user authentication into the platform is delegated to identity providers: Microsoft Entra ID (per-tenant) for all customers, Google Workspace OAuth for the platform tenant, and email-and-password for the platform tenant only (an intentional self-serve feature; all dedicated customer tenants enforce SSO via their identity provider). Multi-factor authentication is enforced at the identity-provider level. Sessions expire after seven days with one-day refresh intervals.
- Engineer access to the Azure environment is delegated to Microsoft Entra ID with Privileged Identity Management (PIM). The AKS cluster has local admin accounts disabled and uses Entra-backed Kubernetes RBAC. PostgreSQL access requires Entra AD authentication; developer access to a customer-tenant database requires PIM activation of a per-tenant Entra group, and all developer queries are captured by `pgaudit` with the developer's user principal name recorded in the log line prefix.
- Workloads authenticate to Azure resources using federated workload identities (per-tenant Kubernetes service accounts federated to per-purpose Azure user-assigned managed identities). Application secrets are mounted from Azure Key Vault via the Secrets Store CSI Driver.
- Multi-factor authentication is enforced on all employee accounts and on all administrative access to production systems.
- Encryption: TLS 1.2+ in transit; AES-256 at rest (Azure platform-managed encryption with Microsoft-managed keys); application secrets managed in Azure Key Vault. Selected sensitive fields are additionally encrypted at the application layer (see DC 3.4).

### CC7 — System Operations

| Activity | Tooling | Cadence |
|---|---|---|
| Static application security testing | GitHub CodeQL (default setup, configured in repository Code Security settings — evidence in GitHub Security tab) | Weekly + on push to default branch |
| Dependency vulnerability detection | GitHub Dependabot alerts (default setup; auto-update PRs disabled; remediation prioritised via Linear) | Continuous |
| Dynamic application security testing | OWASP ZAP baseline against production (`app.normain.com`) and the development environment (`app-dev.normain.com`) | Weekly |
| Container image scanning | Trivy (scans `normain-api`, `normain-worker`, `normain-tenant-api`; severity floor: HIGH / CRITICAL; results uploaded to GitHub Security tab) | Weekly |
| Pre-merge code quality | Lint, type-check, automated test suite, Terraform format check | Every pull request |
| Production monitoring and alerting | Application logs and traces ingested into BetterStack via OpenTelemetry (180-day retention); availability and CPU saturation monitored via Azure Monitor metric alerts; uptime probing configured in BetterStack | 24/7 |
| Security alert triage | Findings from CodeQL, Dependabot, Trivy, and ZAP surface in the GitHub Security tab; an engineer reviews them and tracks remediation in Linear | Continuous |

### CC8 — Change Management

- All production code changes flow through a pull request with at least one independent reviewer prior to merge into the default branch.
- Deployments are executed by GitHub Actions on every merge to the default branch. Release-relevant changes are captured in pull-request titles and descriptions and summarised in periodic release notes.
- Infrastructure changes are managed via Terraform and follow the same review process as application code.
- Customer-tenant configuration (per-tenant subdomain, environment variables, DNS) is provisioned via an authenticated tenant API and applied by Terraform automation triggered by `repository_dispatch`. The Terraform modules and the platform code remain change-controlled via the same pull-request and code-owner review process; only tenant inventory updates (driven by signed Marketplace and self-serve subscription events) are applied without manual review.

### CC9 — Risk Mitigation: Business Disruption and Vendor Risk

- The Business Continuity and Disaster Recovery Plan is reviewed annually. An annual tabletop exercise is performed (most recent IR tabletop: 2025-07-04; most recent DR tabletop: 2025-07-22).
- Vendors are risk-assessed at onboarding and re-assessed annually or on material change. The vendor inventory is maintained in the Vendor Master Sheet.

